    "lem-qft-example-spin-trace-completeness": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-qft-example-data",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "These are conventions for constructed and formal calculations in this page pair. They are not empirical claims, physical postulates, or existence claims about an interacting continuum theory.\n\nUse the geometric spacetime metric $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$ and the time coordinate $x^0=ct$ in metres. For Clifford algebra and momentum contraction use the separate algebraic metric $h=-\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(1,-1,-1,-1)$; a slash or a square of an energy four-vector uses $h$, never $\\eta$.\n\nA physical energy four-vector is $p=(E,c\\mathbf p_{\\rm phys})$, with each component in joules. Write $M=mc^2$ for rest energy in joules and $p^2=h(p,p)=E^2-c^2|\\mathbf p_{\\rm phys}|^2$. For two such vectors, $s=(p_1+p_2)^2$ is in $\\mathrm J^2$. Natural momentum coordinates are the energy-vector components divided by $\\hbar c$, in $\\mathrm m^{-1}$; they are not substituted into SI formulas without restoring this factor. In the common natural-unit field convention, $\\mu=mc/\\hbar$ is in $\\mathrm m^{-1}$, a scalar field has dimension $\\mathrm m^{-1}$, and a Dirac field has dimension $\\mathrm m^{-3/2}$.\n\nTake SI $e>0$ in coulombs, $\\hbar>0$ in joule-seconds, $c>0$ in metres per second, and $\\epsilon_0=(\\mu_0c^2)^{-1}$. Define\n$$\\alpha=\\frac{e^2}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0\\hbar c},\\qquad g=e\\sqrt{\\frac{\\mu_0c}{\\hbar}}=\\sqrt{4\\pi\\alpha}>0.$$\nHere $g$ is a dimensionless coupling. It is not the metric and not a gyromagnetic factor.\n\nFor gauge notation set $A_{\\rm lower}=(-\\phi/c,\\mathbf A)$, with $A_a$ in tesla-metres and gauge function $\\chi$ in webers. With charge $q$ in coulombs define\n$$D_a=\\partial_a-\\frac{iqA_a}{\\hbar},\\qquad \\psi\\mapsto e^{iq\\chi/\\hbar}\\psi,\\qquad A\\mapsto A+d\\chi.$$\nFor the electron parameter use $q=-e$. The equivalent natural-unit coordinates are $A_{\\rm nat}=A_{\\rm SI}/\\sqrt{\\mu_0\\hbar c}$ and $q_{\\rm nat}=q_{\\rm SI}\\sqrt{\\mu_0c/\\hbar}$.\n\nFinite-matrix examples act on $\\mathbb C^d$ with the operator norm. For Hilbert spaces, use the first-variable-linear pairing $(u,v)_1=v^\\dagger u$; a displayed coordinate contraction $u^\\dagger v$ is second-variable-linear and equals $\\overline{(u,v)_1}$. Thus norms, traces, and modulus-squared amplitudes agree under conversion, although the unsquared pairing is conjugated. No amplitude is assigned a probability by these conventions.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-qft-dirac-projections",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]], the mathematical scalar shell conventions of [[def-qft-scalar-mass-shell]], and the second-variable-linear Hilbert pairing of [[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]]. In the fixed inertial coordinates $x=(x^0,\\mathbf x)$ of [[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]] on the affine Minkowski model [[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]], $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$; these coordinates have length units under [[def-qft-scalar-mass-shell]]. The parameter $\\mu\\ge0$ and momentum coordinate $\\mathbf k$ have inverse-length units, and $\\omega(\\mathbf k)=\\sqrt{|\\mathbf k|^2+\\mu^2}$ has the same units. The parameter is not identified here with a physical rest mass. Matrix $h_D$ has inverse-length units; the dimensionless spin matrices have no physical unit assignment in this mathematical item.\n\nUse the Pauli matrices $\\sigma_1=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix}$, $\\sigma_2=\\begin{pmatrix}0&-i\\\\i&0\\end{pmatrix}$, and $\\sigma_3=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&-1\\end{pmatrix}$. On $\\mathbb C^4=\\mathbb C^2\\oplus\\mathbb C^2$, define\n$$\\beta=\\begin{pmatrix}I_2&0\\\\0&-I_2\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad \\alpha_j=\\begin{pmatrix}0&\\sigma_j\\\\\\sigma_j&0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad \\gamma^0=\\beta,\\qquad \\gamma^j=\\beta\\alpha_j.$$\nFor a spinor column $z\\in\\mathbb C^4$, its Dirac adjoint is $\\bar z=z^\\dagger\\beta$. The Clifford contraction is $\\Gamma(p):=\\gamma^0p^0-\\sum_{j=1}^3\\gamma^jp_j$; this contraction uses the effective matrix $-\\eta$ in the relation $\\{\\gamma^a,\\gamma^b\\}=-2\\eta^{ab}I_4$.\n\nLet $\\mathcal H_D$ be the space of measurable functions $u:\\mathbb R^3\\to\\mathbb C^4$, modulo equality almost everywhere, with\n$$\\|u\\|^2=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}u(\\mathbf k)^\\dagger u(\\mathbf k)\\,\\frac{d^3\\mathbf k}{(2\\pi)^3}<\\infty,$$\nand pairing $\\langle u,v\\rangle=\\int u(\\mathbf k)^\\dagger v(\\mathbf k)\\,d^3\\mathbf k/(2\\pi)^3$. Define the measurable matrix $h_D(\\mathbf k)=\\sum_j\\alpha_j k_j+\\beta\\mu$ and its maximal multiplication operator with domain $D(h_D)=\\{u\\in\\mathcal H_D:h_Du\\in\\mathcal H_D\\}$. For $\\omega(\\mathbf k)>0$, define the matrix fields\n$$P_\\pm(\\mathbf k)=\\frac12\\left(I_4\\pm\\frac{h_D(\\mathbf k)}{\\omega(\\mathbf k)}\\right).$$\nWhen $\\mu=0$ at $\\mathbf k=0$, set $P_+(0)=\\operatorname{diag}(I_2,0)$ and $P_-(0)=\\operatorname{diag}(0,I_2)$. These are complementary rank-two orthogonal projections; the value at this Lebesgue-null point does not change their multiplication operators. Write $M_{P_\\pm}$ for their maximal multiplication operators and $\\mathcal H_\\pm=\\operatorname{ran}M_{P_\\pm}$. The companion matrix lemma on this page proves these are bounded orthogonal projections with closed ranges. The conjugate Hilbert space $\\overline{\\mathcal H_-}$ has vectors $\\bar u$, scalar multiplication $z\\bar u=\\overline{\\bar z u}$, and pairing $\\langle\\bar u,\\bar v\\rangle=\\langle v,u\\rangle$.\n\nUse the sine and cosine definitions of [[def-sine-and-cosine-by-power-series]]. For a length-coordinate increment $t\\in\\mathbb R$ and $\\omega(\\mathbf k)>0$, define the matrix multiplier\n$$W_D(t,\\mathbf k)=\\cos(\\omega t)I_4+i\\frac{\\sin(\\omega t)}{\\omega}h_D(\\mathbf k).$$\nAt the massless apex set $W_D(t,0)=I_4$. Its unitarity, group law and generator identity are proved by the companion matrix lemma on this page.\n\nThe symbols $P_+$ and $P_-$ designate the positive- and negative-energy matrix formulas for this fixed free Dirac operator. This is purely mathematical data; no physical spin-statistics postulate or empirical assertion is included.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Direct multiplication of the displayed Pauli matrices gives $\\sigma_j^2=I_2$, $\\sigma_1\\sigma_2=i\\sigma_3$, $\\sigma_2\\sigma_3=i\\sigma_1$, $\\sigma_3\\sigma_1=i\\sigma_2$, and the reversed products have the opposite signs. Hence $\\sigma_i\\sigma_j=\\delta_{ij}I_2+i\\epsilon_{ijk}\\sigma_k$. Block multiplication then gives $\\alpha_i^\\dagger=\\alpha_i$, $\\beta^\\dagger=\\beta$, $\\alpha_i\\alpha_j=\\delta_{ij}I_4+i\\epsilon_{ijk}\\Sigma_k$, and $\\alpha_i\\beta=-\\beta\\alpha_i$. The explicit blocks have trace zero, so $\\operatorname{Tr}\\gamma^a=0$, and $\\operatorname{Tr}I_4=4$. The Clifford relation is $\\{\\gamma^a,\\gamma^b\\}=2h^{ab}I_4$. Cyclicity of trace follows by expanding matrix entries; averaging $\\operatorname{Tr}(\\gamma^a\\gamma^b)$ with its cyclic rearrangement gives $4h^{ab}$. For the four-factor trace, anticommute $\\gamma^a$ successively past $\\gamma^b,\\gamma^c,\\gamma^d$; cyclicity makes the final four-factor term the negative of the original, yielding $4(h^{ab}h^{cd}-h^{ac}h^{bd}+h^{ad}h^{bc})$. Finally $\\gamma_5^2=I_4$ and $\\gamma_5\\gamma^a=-\\gamma^a\\gamma_5$ by the same Clifford relations, so cyclicity gives $\\operatorname{Tr}(\\gamma^a\\gamma^b\\gamma^c)=-\\operatorname{Tr}(\\gamma^a\\gamma^b\\gamma^c)$, hence the three-factor trace is zero.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "The relations in 1.1 give $H_\\pm^\\dagger=H_\\pm$ and $H_\\pm^2=(|\\mathbf p|^2+M^2)I_4=E^2I_4$: the mixed term cancels by $\\alpha_i\\beta=-\\beta\\alpha_i$. Therefore $P_\\pm^\\dagger=P_\\pm$ and $P_\\pm^2=P_\\pm$. Since $\\operatorname{Tr}\\alpha_i=\\operatorname{Tr}\\beta=0$, $\\operatorname{Tr}H_\\pm=0$ and $\\operatorname{Tr}P_\\pm=2$. A Hermitian projection has eigenvalues only $0$ and $1$, so its rank equals its trace and each range has dimension two. A basis can be selected for this fixed finite-dimensional range by Gaussian elimination and orthonormalized by Gram–Schmidt.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "The chosen bases give $\\sum_s u_su_s^\\dagger=2EP_+=EI_4+H_+$ and $\\sum_s v_sv_s^\\dagger=2EP_-=EI_4+H_-$. Multiplying on the right by $\\beta$ yields $EI_4\\beta+H_+\\beta=E\\beta-\\beta\\boldsymbol\\alpha\\cdot\\mathbf p+MI_4=\\not p+M$ and $EI_4\\beta+H_-\\beta=E\\beta-\\beta\\boldsymbol\\alpha\\cdot\\mathbf p-MI_4=\\not p-M$, using $\\alpha_i\\beta=-\\beta\\alpha_i$ and $\\gamma^j=\\beta\\alpha_j$.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 2.1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "Since $H_+u_s=Eu_s$, multiplication by $\\beta$ gives $(\\not p-M)u_s=0$; since $H_-v_s=Ev_s$, it gives $(\\not p+M)v_s=0$. Also $\\gamma^0(\\gamma^a)^\\dagger\\gamma^0=\\gamma^a$: for $a=0$ this is $\\beta^3=\\beta$, and for spatial $a$ it follows from $(\\gamma^a)^\\dagger=-\\gamma^a$ and anticommutation with $\\beta$. Thus $(\\bar x\\gamma^a y)^*=\\bar y\\gamma^a x$ for all columns $x,y$.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
