    "lem-qft-example-two-body-phase-space": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-qft-example-data",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "These are conventions for constructed and formal calculations in this page pair. They are not empirical claims, physical postulates, or existence claims about an interacting continuum theory.\n\nUse the geometric spacetime metric $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$ and the time coordinate $x^0=ct$ in metres. For Clifford algebra and momentum contraction use the separate algebraic metric $h=-\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(1,-1,-1,-1)$; a slash or a square of an energy four-vector uses $h$, never $\\eta$.\n\nA physical energy four-vector is $p=(E,c\\mathbf p_{\\rm phys})$, with each component in joules. Write $M=mc^2$ for rest energy in joules and $p^2=h(p,p)=E^2-c^2|\\mathbf p_{\\rm phys}|^2$. For two such vectors, $s=(p_1+p_2)^2$ is in $\\mathrm J^2$. Natural momentum coordinates are the energy-vector components divided by $\\hbar c$, in $\\mathrm m^{-1}$; they are not substituted into SI formulas without restoring this factor. In the common natural-unit field convention, $\\mu=mc/\\hbar$ is in $\\mathrm m^{-1}$, a scalar field has dimension $\\mathrm m^{-1}$, and a Dirac field has dimension $\\mathrm m^{-3/2}$.\n\nTake SI $e>0$ in coulombs, $\\hbar>0$ in joule-seconds, $c>0$ in metres per second, and $\\epsilon_0=(\\mu_0c^2)^{-1}$. Define\n$$\\alpha=\\frac{e^2}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0\\hbar c},\\qquad g=e\\sqrt{\\frac{\\mu_0c}{\\hbar}}=\\sqrt{4\\pi\\alpha}>0.$$\nHere $g$ is a dimensionless coupling. It is not the metric and not a gyromagnetic factor.\n\nFor gauge notation set $A_{\\rm lower}=(-\\phi/c,\\mathbf A)$, with $A_a$ in tesla-metres and gauge function $\\chi$ in webers. With charge $q$ in coulombs define\n$$D_a=\\partial_a-\\frac{iqA_a}{\\hbar},\\qquad \\psi\\mapsto e^{iq\\chi/\\hbar}\\psi,\\qquad A\\mapsto A+d\\chi.$$\nFor the electron parameter use $q=-e$. The equivalent natural-unit coordinates are $A_{\\rm nat}=A_{\\rm SI}/\\sqrt{\\mu_0\\hbar c}$ and $q_{\\rm nat}=q_{\\rm SI}\\sqrt{\\mu_0c/\\hbar}$.\n\nFinite-matrix examples act on $\\mathbb C^d$ with the operator norm. For Hilbert spaces, use the first-variable-linear pairing $(u,v)_1=v^\\dagger u$; a displayed coordinate contraction $u^\\dagger v$ is second-variable-linear and equals $\\overline{(u,v)_1}$. Thus norms, traces, and modulus-squared amplitudes agree under conversion, although the unsquared pairing is conjugated. No amplitude is assigned a probability by these conventions.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-qft-example-two-body-phase-space-data",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]] and use the energy-vector, metric, and unit conventions of [[def-qft-example-data]]. A rest-energy parameter $\\mathsf M\\geq0$ is measured in joules. Its positive shell is parameterized by $p=(E,\\mathbf p)$ with $E=\\sqrt{|\\mathbf p|^2+\\mathsf M^2}>0$; the shell measure is $d\\nu_{\\mathsf M}=d^3\\mathbf p/((2\\pi)^3\\,2E)$. The value $\\mathsf M=0$, $\\mathbf p=0$ is excluded.\n\nLet $\\mathsf M_1,\\mathsf M_2\\geq0$ and let $s>(\\mathsf M_1+\\mathsf M_2)^2$. Define\n$$k_*=\\frac{\\sqrt{[s-(\\mathsf M_1+\\mathsf M_2)^2][s-(\\mathsf M_1-\\mathsf M_2)^2]}}{2\\sqrt{s}}, \\qquad E_i^*=\\sqrt{k_*^2+\\mathsf M_i^2}.$$\nBoth factors under the square root are positive, so $k_*>0$. On the Euclidean unit sphere $S^2$, let $d\\Omega$ be the chart surface measure of [[lem-euclidean-chart-measure-agrees-with-polar-surface-measure]]. The center-of-momentum two-body phase-space measure is the finite Borel measure\n$$d\\Phi_2=\\frac{k_*}{16\\pi^2\\sqrt{s}}\\,d\\Omega, \\qquad \\mathbf n\\longmapsto ((E_1^*,k_*\\mathbf n),(E_2^*,-k_*\\mathbf n)).$$\n\nFor future energy four-vectors $p_1,p_2$ with nonnegative rest energies $\\mathsf M_{1,\\mathrm{in}},\\mathsf M_{2,\\mathrm{in}}\\geq0$, define the invariant flux when its radicand is nonnegative by\n$$F(p_1,p_2)=4\\sqrt{(p_1\\cdot p_2)^2-\\mathsf M_{1,\\mathrm{in}}^2\\mathsf M_{2,\\mathrm{in}}^2}, \\qquad p\\cdot q=h(p,q).$$\nLet $\\mathcal S_{\\rm in}$ and $\\mathcal S_{\\rm out}$ be finite nonempty initial and final label sets, respectively, and let $w_i\\geq0$ for $i\\in\\mathcal S_{\\rm in}$ satisfy $\\sum_iw_i=1$. A spin-resolved formal amplitude is a family of dimensionless Borel functions $\\mathcal A_{if}:S^2\\to\\mathbb C$, indexed by $i\\in\\mathcal S_{\\rm in}$ and $f\\in\\mathcal S_{\\rm out}$. Define the Borel function\n$$\\overline{|\\mathcal A(\\mathbf n)|^2}:=\\sum_{i\\in\\mathcal S_{\\rm in}}w_i\\sum_{f\\in\\mathcal S_{\\rm out}}|\\mathcal A_{if}(\\mathbf n)|^2.$$\nFor a final pair, set $S_f=2$ when the two final particles are identical and $S_f=1$ when they are distinguishable. When the incoming flux is positive, $F>0$, define the center-of-momentum differential cross-section coefficient measure by\n$$d\\sigma_{\\mathrm{coeff}}=\\frac{(\\hbar c)^2}{F S_f}\\, \\overline{|\\mathcal A(\\mathbf n)|^2}\\,d\\Phi_2.$$\nFor a parent at rest with energy $\\mathsf M_0>0$ and a specified single-channel Borel formal decay amplitude $\\mathcal G:S^2\\to\\mathbb C$ measured in joules, define the extended nonnegative coefficient\n$$\\Gamma_{\\mathrm{coeff}}=\\frac{1}{2\\hbar\\mathsf M_0 S_f} \\int_{S^2}|\\mathcal G(\\mathbf n)|^2\\,d\\Phi_2.$$\nIt is a finite rate coefficient when this integral is finite; otherwise the displayed integral has value $+\\infty$.\nThese are mathematical coefficient maps for the explicitly specified formal amplitudes; their definitions assert no exact interacting scattering operator or decay law.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "4.1",
            "5.1",
            "6.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-c-one-change-of-variables-for-nonnegative-borel-functions-via-radon-uniqueness",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. Let $m\\ge1$, let U,V be open subsets of $\\mathbb R^m$, and let $T:U\\to V$ be a $C^1$ diffeomorphism. For every nonnegative Borel $h:V\\to[0,\\infty]$, $\\int_V h(y)\\,dy=\\int_U h(T(x))|\\det DT(x)|\\,dx$, with equality in $[0,\\infty]$ and $0\\cdot\\infty=0$. This statement concerns Borel h; no completed-measurable substitution is asserted.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-euclidean-chart-measure-agrees-with-polar-surface-measure",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ and $n\\ge2$. The chart surface measure on $S^{n-1}$ equals the polar measure $\\sigma$. Orthogonal transformations preserve it. The map $\\omega\\mapsto a+R\\omega$, $R>0$, multiplies surface measure by $R^{n-1}$, and $|S^{n-1}|=n|B_1|$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Let $a=(a_0,\\mathbf a)$ be any future unit vector, so $a_0>0$ and $a_0^2-|\\mathbf a|^2=1$. If $\\mathbf a=0$, put $B=I$. Otherwise put $r=|\\mathbf a|$, $\\mathbf n=\\mathbf a/r$, and define $B$ to act by $\\begin{pmatrix}a_0&r\\\\r&a_0\\end{pmatrix}$ on $\\operatorname{span}(e_0,\\mathbf n)$ and by the identity on its spatial orthogonal complement. In both cases $B$ preserves $h$, has determinant one, preserves the future cone, and sends $e_0$ to $a$. For any proper orthochronous $h$-Lorentz map $\\Lambda$, apply this construction to $a=\\Lambda e_0$; then $B^{-1}\\Lambda$ fixes $e_0$ and restricts to a proper spatial rotation on $e_0^\\perp$. Thus every such $\\Lambda$ is a boost followed by a spatial rotation. The construction also gives, for every future unit vector $a$, an inverse boost taking $a$ to $e_0$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "algebra",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "A spatial rotation has Jacobian one and leaves $E$ fixed. By step 1.1, it remains to check a boost directed along the first spatial axis. For that boost, $p'_1=a_0p_1+rE$ and $E'=a_0E+rp_1$, so $\\partial p'_1/\\partial p_1=a_0+rp_1/E=E'/E>0$; the other spatial coordinates are unchanged. The inverse boost is obtained by replacing $r$ with $-r$, so this spatial map is a $C^1$ diffeomorphism. By [F3], $d^3\\mathbf p'=(E'/E)d^3\\mathbf p$ in every nonnegative Borel integral, and therefore $d^3\\mathbf p'/(2E')=d^3\\mathbf p/(2E)$. This proves shell-measure invariance under the boost and, using the rotation factorization, under every proper orthochronous $h$-Lorentz map.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "F3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Apply the boost construction in step 1.1 to the future unit vector $P/\\sqrt{s}$; its inverse takes $P/\\sqrt{s}$ to $e_0$. Step 2.1 preserves each shell measure, and $|\\det\\Lambda|=1$ preserves the four-dimensional delta under this change of frame. Thus it suffices to evaluate at $P=(\\sqrt{s},\\mathbf0)$. Integrating the spatial delta first sets $\\mathbf k_2=-\\mathbf k_1$ and gives the remaining radial measure\n$$\\frac{1}{4(2\\pi)^2E_1(r)E_2(r)}r^2\\,dr\\,d\\Omega\\,\\delta(\\sqrt{s}-E_1(r)-E_2(r)).$$\nHere $d^3\\mathbf k_1=r^2dr\\,d\\Omega$ by [F4].",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "For $r>0$, $E_1(r)+E_2(r)$ is strictly increasing, with derivative $r(1/E_1+1/E_2)>0$. The threshold assumption gives a unique root $k_*>0$. At this root, $E_1+E_2=\\sqrt{s}$ and $E_1-E_2=(\\mathsf M_1^2-\\mathsf M_2^2)/\\sqrt{s}$, so $E_1=(s+\\mathsf M_1^2-\\mathsf M_2^2)/(2\\sqrt{s})$, $E_2=(s-\\mathsf M_1^2+\\mathsf M_2^2)/(2\\sqrt{s})$, and\n$$k_*^2=E_1^2-\\mathsf M_1^2=\\frac{[s-(\\mathsf M_1+\\mathsf M_2)^2][s-(\\mathsf M_1-\\mathsf M_2)^2]}{4s}.$$\nThe derivative at the root is $k_*\\sqrt{s}/(E_1E_2)$. Applying [F3] to the one-dimensional increasing change of variable evaluates the energy delta in step 3.1, giving $d\\Phi_2=k_*d\\Omega/(16\\pi^2\\sqrt{s})$. By [F4], $\\int_{S^2}d\\Omega=2\\pi\\int_{-1}^1du=4\\pi$, hence $\\int d\\Phi_2=k_*/(4\\pi\\sqrt{s})$. For equal daughter energies $\\mathsf M$, this gives $k_*=\\sqrt{s}\\beta/2$ and $\\int d\\Phi_2=\\beta/(8\\pi)$, where $\\beta=\\sqrt{1-4\\mathsf M^2/s}$.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 3.1",
            "given",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "claim": "In the incoming center-of-momentum frame from step 3.1, write $p_1=(E_1,\\mathbf p_*)$ and $p_2=(E_2,-\\mathbf p_*)$, so $E_1+E_2=\\sqrt{s}$. The mass-shell equations give $p_1\\cdot p_2=E_1E_2+p_*^2$ and $\\mathsf M_{i,\\mathrm{in}}^2=E_i^2-p_*^2$. Direct expansion yields\n$$(p_1\\cdot p_2)^2-\\mathsf M_{1,\\mathrm{in}}^2\\mathsf M_{2,\\mathrm{in}}^2=(E_1E_2+p_*^2)^2-(E_1^2-p_*^2)(E_2^2-p_*^2)=p_*^2(E_1+E_2)^2=sp_*^2,$$\nso [F2] gives $F=4p_*\\sqrt{s}$. If the incoming rest energies are equal to $\\mathsf M_{\\mathrm{in}}$, then $E_1=E_2=\\sqrt{s}/2$ and $p_*^2=s/4-\\mathsf M_{\\mathrm{in}}^2$; since $p_*>0$, $p_*=\\sqrt{s}\\beta_{\\mathrm{in}}/2$ and $F=2s\\beta_{\\mathrm{in}}$. Combining the phase-space density from step 4.1 with [F2]'s coefficient map yields\n$$\\frac{d\\sigma_{\\mathrm{coeff}}}{d\\Omega}=\\frac{(\\hbar c)^2}{64\\pi^2s}\\frac{k_*}{p_*}\\frac{\\overline{|\\mathcal A|^2}}{S_f}.$$",
          "step": "5.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 3.1",
            "step 4.1",
            "given",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-6-1",
          "claim": "For a parent at rest with energy $\\mathsf M_0>2\\mathsf M$ and two equal-mass daughters, step 4.1 gives $\\int d\\Phi_2=\\sqrt{1-4\\mathsf M^2/\\mathsf M_0^2}/(8\\pi)$. The decay coefficient map in [F2] divides $|G_E|^2\\int d\\Phi_2$ by $2\\hbar\\mathsf M_0S_f$; for identical daughters $S_f=2$, this is $|G_E|^2\\sqrt{1-4\\mathsf M^2/\\mathsf M_0^2}/(32\\pi\\hbar\\mathsf M_0)$. Its units are $\\mathrm J^2/(\\mathrm{J\\,s\\,J})=\\mathrm s^{-1}$. No survival law enters this coefficient calculation.",
          "step": "6.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 4.1",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
