    "thm-qft-example-massive-tree-annihilation": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-qft-example-data",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "These are conventions for constructed and formal calculations in this page pair. They are not empirical claims, physical postulates, or existence claims about an interacting continuum theory.\n\nUse the geometric spacetime metric $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$ and the time coordinate $x^0=ct$ in metres. For Clifford algebra and momentum contraction use the separate algebraic metric $h=-\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(1,-1,-1,-1)$; a slash or a square of an energy four-vector uses $h$, never $\\eta$.\n\nA physical energy four-vector is $p=(E,c\\mathbf p_{\\rm phys})$, with each component in joules. Write $M=mc^2$ for rest energy in joules and $p^2=h(p,p)=E^2-c^2|\\mathbf p_{\\rm phys}|^2$. For two such vectors, $s=(p_1+p_2)^2$ is in $\\mathrm J^2$. Natural momentum coordinates are the energy-vector components divided by $\\hbar c$, in $\\mathrm m^{-1}$; they are not substituted into SI formulas without restoring this factor. In the common natural-unit field convention, $\\mu=mc/\\hbar$ is in $\\mathrm m^{-1}$, a scalar field has dimension $\\mathrm m^{-1}$, and a Dirac field has dimension $\\mathrm m^{-3/2}$.\n\nTake SI $e>0$ in coulombs, $\\hbar>0$ in joule-seconds, $c>0$ in metres per second, and $\\epsilon_0=(\\mu_0c^2)^{-1}$. Define\n$$\\alpha=\\frac{e^2}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0\\hbar c},\\qquad g=e\\sqrt{\\frac{\\mu_0c}{\\hbar}}=\\sqrt{4\\pi\\alpha}>0.$$\nHere $g$ is a dimensionless coupling. It is not the metric and not a gyromagnetic factor.\n\nFor gauge notation set $A_{\\rm lower}=(-\\phi/c,\\mathbf A)$, with $A_a$ in tesla-metres and gauge function $\\chi$ in webers. With charge $q$ in coulombs define\n$$D_a=\\partial_a-\\frac{iqA_a}{\\hbar},\\qquad \\psi\\mapsto e^{iq\\chi/\\hbar}\\psi,\\qquad A\\mapsto A+d\\chi.$$\nFor the electron parameter use $q=-e$. The equivalent natural-unit coordinates are $A_{\\rm nat}=A_{\\rm SI}/\\sqrt{\\mu_0\\hbar c}$ and $q_{\\rm nat}=q_{\\rm SI}\\sqrt{\\mu_0c/\\hbar}$.\n\nFinite-matrix examples act on $\\mathbb C^d$ with the operator norm. For Hilbert spaces, use the first-variable-linear pairing $(u,v)_1=v^\\dagger u$; a displayed coordinate contraction $u^\\dagger v$ is second-variable-linear and equals $\\overline{(u,v)_1}$. Thus norms, traces, and modulus-squared amplitudes agree under conversion, although the unsquared pairing is conjugated. No amplitude is assigned a probability by these conventions.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-qft-example-spin-trace-completeness",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Use the finite Dirac matrices and adjoint of [[def-qft-dirac-projections]], and the separate geometric metric $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$ and algebraic contraction $h=-\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(1,-1,-1,-1)$ of [[def-qft-example-data]]. Write $p=(E,\\mathbf p)$ for an energy four-vector with components in joules, $M\\geq0$ for a rest-energy parameter in joules, and $E=\\sqrt{|\\mathbf p|^2+M^2}>0$. In the natural-length variables of the Dirac definition, $\\mathbf k=\\mathbf p/(\\hbar c)$, $\\mu=M/(\\hbar c)$, and $\\omega=E/(\\hbar c)$.\n\nLet $\\beta=\\gamma^0$, $\\alpha_j=\\beta\\gamma^j$, $\\Sigma_j=\\operatorname{diag}(\\sigma_j,\\sigma_j)$, and $\\gamma_5=i\\gamma^0\\gamma^1\\gamma^2\\gamma^3$. Define $H_\\pm=\\boldsymbol\\alpha\\cdot\\mathbf p\\pm\\beta M$ and $P_\\pm=(I_4+H_\\pm/E)/2$. Each $P_\\pm$ is a Hermitian rank-two projection. Choose orthonormal bases $\\xi_{\\pm,1},\\xi_{\\pm,2}$ of their ranges and put $u_s=\\sqrt{2E}\\xi_{+,s}$ and $v_s=\\sqrt{2E}\\xi_{-,s}$ for $s=1,2$. With $\\bar z=z^\\dagger\\beta$ and $\\not p=\\gamma^0E-\\sum_j\\gamma^jp_j$, the following hold:\n\n$$\\operatorname{Tr}I_4=4,\\quad \\operatorname{Tr}\\gamma^a=0,\\quad \\operatorname{Tr}(\\gamma^a\\gamma^b)=4h^{ab},\\quad \\operatorname{Tr}(\\gamma^a\\gamma^b\\gamma^c)=0,$$\n\n$$\\operatorname{Tr}(\\gamma^a\\gamma^b\\gamma^c\\gamma^d) =4(h^{ab}h^{cd}-h^{ac}h^{bd}+h^{ad}h^{bc}), \\qquad \\sum_s u_s\\bar u_s=\\not p+M,\\quad \\sum_s v_s\\bar v_s=\\not p-M.$$\n\nThe columns satisfy $(\\not p-M)u_s=0$ and $(\\not p+M)v_s=0$. For all $x,y\\in\\mathbb C^4$, complex conjugation of a Dirac current is $\\overline{\\bar x\\gamma^a y}=\\bar y\\gamma^a x$. The Countable Choice hypothesis written in the current draft [[def-qft-dirac-projections]] is retained as a supplier assumption; this finite-dimensional proof makes no additional choice use.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "2.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-qft-example-two-body-phase-space",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]], and use [[def-qft-example-data]] and [[def-qft-example-two-body-phase-space-data]]. A proper orthochronous linear $h$-Lorentz map is a real linear $\\Lambda$ with $\\Lambda^Th\\Lambda=h$, $\\det\\Lambda=1$, and $\\Lambda$ preserving the future cone $E>0$. For future energy four-vectors in the algebraic metric $h=(+,-,-,-)$, the shell measure $d^3\\mathbf p/((2\\pi)^3\\,2E)$ is invariant under every such map. Write $E_i(r)=\\sqrt{r^2+\\mathsf M_i^2}$. Let $f$ be a nonnegative Borel function on the product of the positive shells of [[def-qft-example-two-body-phase-space-data]]. The standard two-body on-shell integral\n$$(2\\pi)^4\\int d\\nu_{\\mathsf M_1}(k_1)d\\nu_{\\mathsf M_2}(k_2)\\, \\delta^{(4)}(P-k_1-k_2)\\,f(k_1,k_2)$$\nis interpreted by first evaluating the spatial delta in $k_2$, then the one-dimensional energy delta by the increasing radial change of variable $r\\mapsto E_1(r)+E_2(r)$. Explicitly, after the spatial evaluation its radial action is $\\int_0^\\infty g(r)\\delta(\\sqrt{s}-E_1(r)-E_2(r))\\,dr=g(k_*)/(d(E_1+E_2)/dr|_{k_*})$ for nonnegative Borel $g$; this is the pushforward evaluation under that $C^1$ diffeomorphism, not a product of distributions. It equals $\\int f\\,d\\Phi_2$ from the Definition. The threshold root is $k_*$ in that Definition, and\n$$\\int d\\Phi_2=\\frac{k_*}{4\\pi\\sqrt{s}}.$$\nFor equal rest energies $\\mathsf M_1=\\mathsf M_2=\\mathsf M$, set $\\beta=\\sqrt{1-4\\mathsf M^2/s}$; then $k_*=\\sqrt{s}\\beta/2$ and $\\int d\\Phi_2=\\beta/(8\\pi)$. For future incoming energy four-vectors $p_1,p_2$ with $p_1+p_2=P$ and nonnegative rest energies $\\mathsf M_{1,\\mathrm{in}},\\mathsf M_{2,\\mathrm{in}}\\geq0$, let $p_*$ be their center-of-momentum spatial momentum magnitude. Their invariant flux is $F=4p_*\\sqrt{s}$. For equal incoming rest energies $\\mathsf M_{\\mathrm{in}}\\geq0$ with $s\\geq4\\mathsf M_{\\mathrm{in}}^2$, define $\\beta_{\\mathrm{in}}=\\sqrt{1-4\\mathsf M_{\\mathrm{in}}^2/s}$; then $p_*=\\sqrt{s}\\beta_{\\mathrm{in}}/2$ and $F=2s\\beta_{\\mathrm{in}}$. For finite nonempty incoming and outgoing label sets, normalized initial weights, and the Borel amplitude family of the Definition, whenever $F>0$ the cross-section coefficient map gives\n$$\\frac{d\\sigma_{\\mathrm{coeff}}}{d\\Omega} =\\frac{(\\hbar c)^2}{64\\pi^2s}\\, \\frac{k_*}{p_*}\\, \\frac{\\overline{|\\mathcal A|^2}}{S_f}.$$\nFor a parent at rest with energy $\\mathsf M_0>2\\mathsf M$ decaying to two equal rest-energy daughters with constant amplitude $G_E$ in joules, the rate coefficient is $|G_E|^2\\sqrt{1-4\\mathsf M^2/\\mathsf M_0^2}/(32\\pi\\hbar\\mathsf M_0)$ when the daughters are identical. This is a coefficient calculation, not a claim of exponential survival.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L1",
          "source": "lem-qft-qed-formal-matrix-ward",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $K(z;\\zeta)=K_0(\\zeta)+zV(\\zeta)$ and $S(z;\\zeta)$ be the finite-dimensional formal matrices of [[def-qft-qed-formal-ring]], with $d\\ge1$, $K_0(\\zeta)$ invertible, and both coefficient matrices covariant under the vertex phase angles of [[def-qft-qed-finite-graph-gauge-data]]. Then $S$ is a two-sided inverse of $K$ in $M_d(\\mathbb C\\llbracket z\\rrbracket)$, and\n$$ S(z;\\zeta^\\theta)=D_\\theta S(z;\\zeta)D_\\theta^{-1} $$\ncoefficientwise. For a differentiable phase path $D_s=e^{is\\Theta}$, where the real vertex-angle path is $s\\theta$, the variations satisfy\n$$ \\delta K=i[\\Theta,K],\\qquad \\delta S=i[\\Theta,S]=-S(\\delta K)S . $$\nFor ordinary finite matrices $A,B\\in GL_d(\\mathbb C)$, the corresponding resolvent identity is $B^{-1}(B-A)A^{-1}=A^{-1}-B^{-1}$. These are formal or finite algebraic statements; they assert no convergence of a perturbation series, continuum limit, or loop Ward identity.",
          "uses": [
            "2.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "By [F2], the spin sums reduce the squared-current factors to\n$$L_e^{ab}=\\frac14\\operatorname{Tr}[(\\not p_2-m)\\gamma^a(\\not p_1+m)\\gamma^b], \\qquad L_{\\mu,ab}=\\operatorname{Tr}[(\\not k_1+M)\\gamma_a(\\not k_2-M)\\gamma_b].$$\nThe odd traces vanish. Expanding the even terms with [F2] and using $p_1\\cdot p_2+m^2=s/2$ and $k_1\\cdot k_2+M^2=s/2$ gives\n$$L_e^{ab}=p_2^ap_1^b+p_2^bp_1^a-\\frac{s}{2}h^{ab}, \\qquad L_{\\mu,ab}=4(k_{1a}k_{2b}+k_{1b}k_{2a}-\\frac{s}{2}h_{ab}).$$\nThese mass-shell dot products follow from $(p_1+p_2)^2=(k_1+k_2)^2=s$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "A1",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Contracting the tensors gives\n$$L_e^{ab}L_{\\mu,ab} =4\\{2[(p_2\\cdot k_1)(p_1\\cdot k_2)+(p_2\\cdot k_2)(p_1\\cdot k_1)] +s^2-s(p_1\\cdot p_2+k_1\\cdot k_2)\\}.$$\nThe two cross-metric terms contribute $-s(p_1\\cdot p_2+k_1\\cdot k_2)$, while the metric-metric term contributes $s^2$; substituting $p_1\\cdot p_2=(s-2m^2)/2$ and $k_1\\cdot k_2=(s-2M^2)/2$ reduces the last two terms to $s(m^2+M^2)$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "claim": "The finite matrix hypothesis [L1] supplies coefficientwise inverse and phase-covariance identities only for its specified finite graph kernel. For the separate amplitude in [A1], the spinor equations from [F2] give $\\bar v(p_2)\\not p_2=-m\\bar v(p_2)$ and $\\not p_1u(p_1)=mu(p_1)$, so their sum contracted with the incoming current is zero. Likewise $\\bar u(k_1)\\not k_1=M\\bar u(k_1)$ and $\\not k_2v(k_2)=-Mv(k_2)$, so the outgoing current is transverse to $P=k_1+k_2$. A longitudinal propagator term is therefore multiplied by two zero contractions. No broader tree-current claim is taken from [L1].",
          "step": "2.2",
          "inputs": [
            "L1",
            "A1",
            "F2",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "In the center-of-momentum frame take $|\\mathbf p_1|=\\sqrt{s}\\beta_e/2$, $|\\mathbf k_1|=\\sqrt{s}\\beta_\\mu/2$, and let $\\theta$ be the angle between them. The four cross-dot-products in step 2.1 are $s/4\\pm|\\mathbf p_1||\\mathbf k_1|\\cos\\theta$, with each sign occurring twice. Substitution into step 2.1 and multiplication by $g^4/s^2$ gives $\\overline{|\\mathcal M|^2}=g^4[3-\\beta_e^2-\\beta_\\mu^2+\\beta_e^2\\beta_\\mu^2\\cos^2\\theta]$. The resulting function of $\\theta$ is continuous and therefore Borel; the spin-label sums are finite by [F2]. The strict threshold gives positive $\\beta_e,\\beta_\\mu$, and [F3] gives incoming flux $F=2s\\beta_e>0$.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 2.1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "For distinguishable final particles, [F3] gives $d\\Phi_2/d\\Omega=\\beta_\\mu/(32\\pi^2)$ and $F=2s\\beta_e$. Substituting the squared amplitude from step 3.1 into [F3] yields the displayed differential coefficient after using $g^4=(4\\pi\\alpha)^2$. Integrating uses $\\int_{S^2}d\\Omega=4\\pi$ and $\\int_{S^2}\\cos^2\\theta\\,d\\Omega=4\\pi/3$, giving the displayed total coefficient.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 3.1",
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "claim": "For $\\delta=4m^2/s\\leq1/2$, set $B_0=2-\\beta_\\mu^2+\\beta_\\mu^2\\cos^2\\theta$ and $Q=1-\\beta_\\mu^2\\cos^2\\theta$. Then $B_0\\geq1$, $0\\leq Q\\leq1$, and the ratio of the full coefficient to its $m=0$ value is $R=(B_0+\\delta Q)/(B_0\\sqrt{1-\\delta})\\geq1$. Also\n$$R-1=\\frac1{\\sqrt{1-\\delta}}-1+ \\frac{\\delta Q}{B_0\\sqrt{1-\\delta}} \\leq2\\sqrt2\\,\\delta\\leq4\\delta,$$\nbecause $1/\\sqrt{1-\\delta}-1\\leq\\sqrt2\\,\\delta$ and $B_0\\sqrt{1-\\delta}\\geq1/\\sqrt2$. This compares only the two formal tree coefficients.",
          "step": "5.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 4.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
