    "ex-qftmath-x05": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "cor-qft-example-scalar-tree-coefficients",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $m\\geq0$ be a scalar rest energy in joules and $s>4m^2$. For one scalar initial label of weight $1$ and one specified final channel, take the dimensionless real quartic tree amplitude $\\mathcal A=-\\lambda$. The coefficient map of [[lem-qft-example-two-body-phase-space]] gives\n$$\\frac{d\\sigma_{\\mathrm{coeff}}}{d\\Omega} =\\frac{\\lambda^2(\\hbar c)^2}{128\\pi^2s}, \\qquad \\sigma_{\\mathrm{coeff}}=\\frac{\\lambda^2(\\hbar c)^2}{32\\pi s}$$\nfor identical final scalars integrated over the full labeled sphere. For distinguishable final scalars the two coefficients are twice these values.\n\nFor a parent at rest with energy $\\mathsf M_0>2m$ decaying through a specified single channel to two identical scalars of rest energy $m$ under a constant amplitude $G_E$ in joules, the rate coefficient is\n$$\\Gamma_{\\mathrm{coeff}} =\\frac{|G_E|^2}{32\\pi\\hbar\\mathsf M_0} \\sqrt{1-\\frac{4m^2}{\\mathsf M_0^2}}.$$\nThis result concerns the defined formal coefficients. It does not assert an exact exponential survival law.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-qft-example-two-body-phase-space",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]], and use [[def-qft-example-data]] and [[def-qft-example-two-body-phase-space-data]]. A proper orthochronous linear $h$-Lorentz map is a real linear $\\Lambda$ with $\\Lambda^Th\\Lambda=h$, $\\det\\Lambda=1$, and $\\Lambda$ preserving the future cone $E>0$. For future energy four-vectors in the algebraic metric $h=(+,-,-,-)$, the shell measure $d^3\\mathbf p/((2\\pi)^3\\,2E)$ is invariant under every such map. Write $E_i(r)=\\sqrt{r^2+\\mathsf M_i^2}$. Let $f$ be a nonnegative Borel function on the product of the positive shells of [[def-qft-example-two-body-phase-space-data]]. The standard two-body on-shell integral\n$$(2\\pi)^4\\int d\\nu_{\\mathsf M_1}(k_1)d\\nu_{\\mathsf M_2}(k_2)\\, \\delta^{(4)}(P-k_1-k_2)\\,f(k_1,k_2)$$\nis interpreted by first evaluating the spatial delta in $k_2$, then the one-dimensional energy delta by the increasing radial change of variable $r\\mapsto E_1(r)+E_2(r)$. Explicitly, after the spatial evaluation its radial action is $\\int_0^\\infty g(r)\\delta(\\sqrt{s}-E_1(r)-E_2(r))\\,dr=g(k_*)/(d(E_1+E_2)/dr|_{k_*})$ for nonnegative Borel $g$; this is the pushforward evaluation under that $C^1$ diffeomorphism, not a product of distributions. It equals $\\int f\\,d\\Phi_2$ from the Definition. The threshold root is $k_*$ in that Definition, and\n$$\\int d\\Phi_2=\\frac{k_*}{4\\pi\\sqrt{s}}.$$\nFor equal rest energies $\\mathsf M_1=\\mathsf M_2=\\mathsf M$, set $\\beta=\\sqrt{1-4\\mathsf M^2/s}$; then $k_*=\\sqrt{s}\\beta/2$ and $\\int d\\Phi_2=\\beta/(8\\pi)$. For future incoming energy four-vectors $p_1,p_2$ with $p_1+p_2=P$ and nonnegative rest energies $\\mathsf M_{1,\\mathrm{in}},\\mathsf M_{2,\\mathrm{in}}\\geq0$, let $p_*$ be their center-of-momentum spatial momentum magnitude. Their invariant flux is $F=4p_*\\sqrt{s}$. For equal incoming rest energies $\\mathsf M_{\\mathrm{in}}\\geq0$ with $s\\geq4\\mathsf M_{\\mathrm{in}}^2$, define $\\beta_{\\mathrm{in}}=\\sqrt{1-4\\mathsf M_{\\mathrm{in}}^2/s}$; then $p_*=\\sqrt{s}\\beta_{\\mathrm{in}}/2$ and $F=2s\\beta_{\\mathrm{in}}$. For finite nonempty incoming and outgoing label sets, normalized initial weights, and the Borel amplitude family of the Definition, whenever $F>0$ the cross-section coefficient map gives\n$$\\frac{d\\sigma_{\\mathrm{coeff}}}{d\\Omega} =\\frac{(\\hbar c)^2}{64\\pi^2s}\\, \\frac{k_*}{p_*}\\, \\frac{\\overline{|\\mathcal A|^2}}{S_f}.$$\nFor a parent at rest with energy $\\mathsf M_0>2\\mathsf M$ decaying to two equal rest-energy daughters with constant amplitude $G_E$ in joules, the rate coefficient is $|G_E|^2\\sqrt{1-4\\mathsf M^2/\\mathsf M_0^2}/(32\\pi\\hbar\\mathsf M_0)$ when the daughters are identical. This is a coefficient calculation, not a claim of exponential survival.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Setting $m=0$ in [F1] gives the identical-final total scattering coefficient $\\lambda^2(\\hbar c)^2/(32\\pi s)$; the distinguishable coefficient is twice this value. The two differential coefficients have the corresponding $1/(128\\pi^2s)$ and $1/(64\\pi^2s)$ factors.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Setting the daughter rest energy to zero in [F1] gives $\\Gamma_{\\mathrm{coeff}}=|G_E|^2/(32\\pi\\hbar\\mathsf M_0)$. Since $G_E$ and $\\mathsf M_0$ are in joules and $\\hbar$ is in joule-seconds, the units reduce to $\\mathrm s^{-1}$. This coefficient does not determine a survival-time law.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
