    "ex-qftmath-x09": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-qft-example-dirac-pauli-square",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $m>0$ be a particle mass in kilograms, $q\\in\\mathbb R$ its charge in coulombs, and $A:\\mathbb R^3\\to\\mathbb R^3$ a smooth spatial vector potential with components in tesla-metres. Set $B_k=\\epsilon_{kij}\\partial_iA_j$ and define on the common core $\\mathcal C=C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb C^4)$\n$$\\pi_i=-i\\hbar\\partial_i-qA_i,\\qquad H_D=c\\sum_i\\alpha_i\\pi_i+\\beta mc^2,\\qquad \\Sigma_i=\\operatorname{diag}(\\sigma_i,\\sigma_i).$$\nHere the matrices are those of [[lem-qft-example-spin-trace-completeness]] and $\\hbar,c$ have the SI conventions of [[def-qft-example-data]]. These differential maps preserve $\\mathcal C$, and on $\\mathcal C$,\n$$[\\pi_i,\\pi_j]=iq\\hbar\\epsilon_{ijk}B_k,\\qquad H_D^2=m^2c^4I_4+c^2(\\boldsymbol\\pi^2-q\\hbar\\boldsymbol\\Sigma\\cdot\\mathbf B).$$\nIf the formal first-order square-root coefficient is written as $mc^2+K/(2m)$ with $K=\\boldsymbol\\pi^2-q\\hbar\\boldsymbol\\Sigma\\cdot\\mathbf B$, then its spin term is $-(q/m)\\mathbf S\\cdot\\mathbf B$, where $\\mathbf S=\\hbar\\boldsymbol\\Sigma/2$. Under the coefficient convention $H_{\\mathrm{mag}}=-\\boldsymbol\\mu_{\\mathrm{mag}}\\cdot\\mathbf B$ and $\\boldsymbol\\mu_{\\mathrm{mag}}=g_s(q/(2m))\\mathbf S$, equality of the formal coefficients as a linear function of $q$ gives $g_s=2$ for the minimal tree-level term. No magnetic self-adjoint closure, full spectrum, operator square-root theorem, radiative correction, or empirical prediction is asserted.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "With $q=-e$, the supplier's term is $(e/m)S_3B_0$. Since $\\sigma_3$ has eigenvalues $\\pm1$, $S_3$ has eigenvalues $\\pm\\hbar/2$, so the two coefficients are $\\pm e\\hbar B_0/(2m)=\\pm\\mu_BB_0$, where $\\mu_B=e\\hbar/(2m)$. Their gap is $e\\hbar B_0/m$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Dividing that energy gap by $\\hbar$ gives angular frequency $eB_0/m$, and dividing by $2\\pi$ gives cyclic frequency $\\nu_s=eB_0/(2\\pi m)$. The classical cyclotron coefficient for charge magnitude $e$ in the same uniform field is $\\nu_c=eB_0/(2\\pi m)$ by its defining relation. This compares formal coefficients only; it is not a full Landau-spectrum result or an empirical prediction.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
